glow notes:
- both glows are driven by m_bPowerupMode on the client (flag: CCaptureFlag::ShouldHideGlowEffect,
  players: C_TFPlayer::ShouldShowPowerupGlowEffect), so zeroing the prop removes them
- the powerup glow mirrors the client rules: enemy rune carriers below 30% health for observers
  without a rune, and every non stealthed enemy within 1500 units of a charged Supernova
- the client rules require the local player to be alive; here dead players and spectators see the
    glows too, using their spectator target (m_hObserverTarget) as the viewpoint for distance
- bots, replay and SourceTV are skipped as observers, so no tf_glow entities are spent on them
- the Supernova case reads m_flRuneCharge (client side CTFPlayerShared::IsRuneCharged
  compares it against exactly 100)
- every glow uses mode 0 (draw occluded and visible). mode 2 (visible only) still drew through
    walls in practice, so occlusion is decided by the server line of sight trace instead
- the line of sight trace is needed for that reason: eligibility alone would reveal a low health
    rune carrier anywhere on the map. it costs one trace per candidate pair per refresh
- each carrier uses at most two player-glow entities: one team/health color shared by eligible
    observers without a rune, and one yellow glow shared by eligible charged-Supernova observers
- a shared glow is destroyed and recreated when its recipient set changes; merely stopping
    transmission would leave its client-side glow attached to the still-visible carrier
- a creation failure sticks at -1 so it is not retried (and logged) every refresh; it is cleared
    once the carrier stops qualifying
- tf_glow normally uses FL_EDICT_ALWAYS, which bypasses SDKHook_SetTransmit; our glows use
    full transmit checks and override UpdateTransmitState so the recipient filter runs
- the stolen flag glow is only transmitted to the team the flag belongs to, because that is the
  only team CCaptureFlag::ShouldHideGlowEffect blinds while the flag is stolen (everyone else still
  gets the engine outline), so the two never double up
- m_bGlowEnabled on the flag must be left alone: ShouldHideGlowEffect starts with !IsGlowEnabled(),
  and C_TFPlayer::ItemPostFrame uses that same function to hide the *carrier's* glow, so zeroing it
  silently removes the intel carrier outline as well
- the carrier outline stays engine driven: ShouldHideGlowEffect hides it from the team that owns the
  flag, so an enemy carrying your intel has no outline for you (stock CTF) while their team sees it
  health coloured. Powerup mode shows it to everyone; that is intentionally not restored, so adding
  it later means a second tf_glow on the carrier (flag owner entity) filtered to the flag's team

